arXiv:2509.13178v1 [cs.LG] 16 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks 
<br class="ltx_break">over Hilbert Spaces<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span> Email to: cbattiloro@hsph.harvard.edu</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Claudio Battiloro
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Andrea Cavallo
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Elvin Isufi
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Covariance Neural Networks (VNNs) perform graph convolutions on the empirical covariance matrix of signals defined over finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces, motivated by robustness and transferability properties. Yet, little is known about how these arguments extend to infinite-dimensional Hilbert spaces. In this work, we take a first step by introducing a novel convolutional learning framework for signals defined over infinite-dimensional Hilbert spaces, centered on the (empirical) covariance operator. We constructively define Hilbert coVariance Filters (HVFs) and design Hilbert coVariance Networks (HVNs) as stacks of HVF filterbanks with nonlinear activations. We propose a principled discretization procedure, and we prove that empirical HVFs can recover the Functional PCA (FPCA) of the filtered signals. We then describe the versatility of our framework with examples ranging from multivariate real-valued functions to reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces. Finally, we validate HVNs on both synthetic and real-world time-series classification tasks, showing robust performance compared to MLP and FPCA-based classifiers.</p>
  
</div><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_address"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">address: </span><sup id="id1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>Harvard University  <sup id="id1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Delft University of Technology</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Covariance-based processing underpins several signal processing and machine learning frameworks, such as data whitening, exposing principal directions, and capturing feature interdependence. Its benefits have been shown in a wide plethora of domains, from brain neuroscience to finance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. The covariance matrix spectrum implicitly captures the structure of a dataset via the <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">principal components</span>, and said structure can be exploited via the PCA transform. While PCA learning is broadly adopted <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, its practical implementation suffers from the inherent stochastic perturbations in the empirical covariance matrix estimated from finite data, which gets amplified when in high-dimension/low-sample regimes or with tightly clustered eigenvalues <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. To address this, coVariance Neural Networks (VNNs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> perform graph convolutions using the empirical covariance matrix as graph shift operator, successfully merging the representational power of PCA and the stability/transferability properties of Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. However, VNNs operate only on finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces and little is known about how to generalize their benefits in cases where signals are defined over infinite-dimensional Hilbert spaces. This is a common setting in, for example, time-series processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> or quantum machine learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Related Works.</span> Covariance-based data representation has been extensively studied from a graph signal processing perspective. For example, the works in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> formalized stationarity for graph signals and related the graph Fourier transform with the spectrum of the data covariances. This link has been also exploited in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> for graph-based dimensionality reduction. More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> introduced VNNs, which use the empirical covariance matrix as a graph shift operator, and showed that VNNs are more expressive and stable than PCA. The transferability properties of VNNs have been studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. VNNs have been successfully applied to brain data in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. A variant of VNNs for streaming spatiotemporal signals has been proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, while VNNs with explicit bias mitigation have been proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. The work in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> recently proposed to replace the empirical covariance matrix in VNNs with the empirical covariance density operators to improve user’s control on the stability-discriminability trade-off of the network, and enhance robustness to noise. However, these works consider data only on finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contribution.</span> We introduce a novel convolutional learning framework for random signals defined over general, possibly infinite-dimensional, Hilbert spaces, centered on the covariance operator of such space. We leverage the eigensystem of the covariance operator as a frequency domain to define a Hilbert coVariance Fourier Transform, and then we propose spectral Hilbert coVariance Filters (HVF) to manipulate the signal representation in such spectral domain. We show how one can seamlessly develop Hilbert coVariance Networks (HVNs) as a layered architecture of HVFs and nonlinear activations in the same spirit as GNNs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. We propose a principled discretization procedure to make HVFs and HVNs implementable, and we prove that empirical HVFs can recover the Functional PCA (FPCA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> of the filtered signals. Finally, we numerically validate HVNs on both synthetic and real-world binary classification tasks of time-series, corroborating our findings and showing robustness benefits w.r.t. FPCA and covariance-unaware learning such as MLPs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries and Motivation</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a probability space <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is the sample space (set of all possible outcomes), <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sigma-algebra, i.e., a collection of measurable subsets of <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> treated as events, and <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}:\mathcal{F}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}:\mathcal{F}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is a probability measure, i.e., a function assigning probabilities to the elements in <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider also a separable Hilbert space <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{H},\left\langle\cdot,\,\cdot\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{H},\left\langle\cdot,\,\cdot\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}})</annotation></semantics></math> with inner product <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\langle\cdot,\,\cdot\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\langle\cdot,\,\cdot\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> and norm <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert\cdot\right\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert\cdot\right\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this paper, we work with random variables <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X:\Omega\to\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X:\Omega\to\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> that are square integrable, i.e. they have finite expected squared norm <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\,\left\lVert X\right\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^{2}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\,\left\lVert X\right\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^{2}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the mean <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=\mathbb{E}[X]\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=\mathbb{E}[X]\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. We refer to the elements of <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> as signals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Covariance operator.</span> 
The covariance operator <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the linear map that encodes all directional (mixed) second moments of <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. The covariance operator acts on <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Cv\;=\;\mathbb{E}\Big[\,\underbrace{\left\langle X-\mu,\,v\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}}_{\text{scalar coefficient}}\,(X-\mu)\,\Big]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>scalar coefficient</mtext></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Cv\;=\;\mathbb{E}\Big[\,\underbrace{\left\langle X-\mu,\,v\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}}_{\text{scalar coefficient}}\,(X-\mu)\,\Big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Note that the operator <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is compact and trace-class <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, and hence there exist nonnegative eigenvalues <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\lambda_{\ell}\}_{\ell\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\lambda_{\ell}\}_{\ell\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> and orthonormal eigenfunctions <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{\ell}\}_{\ell\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{\ell}\}_{\ell\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Cv\;=\;\sum_{\ell\geq 1}\lambda_{\ell}\,\left\langle v,\,\phi_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}\,\phi_{\ell}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Cv\;=\;\sum_{\ell\geq 1}\lambda_{\ell}\,\left\langle v,\,\phi_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}\,\phi_{\ell}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hilbert Covariance Fourier Transform (HVFT).</span>
A spectral representation of signals is conventionally tied to a Fourier transform to analyze them.
Given the eigen -system <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\lambda_{\ell},\phi_{\ell})\}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\lambda_{\ell},\phi_{\ell})\}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, we interpret the (ordered) eigenvalues as frequencies, and we define the Hilbert coVariance Fourier Transform (HVFT) of a signal <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> as the projection</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{x}[\ell]\;\triangleq\;\left\langle x,\,\phi_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}},\qquad\ell\geq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≜</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{x}[\ell]\;\triangleq\;\left\langle x,\,\phi_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}},\qquad\ell\geq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, the HVFT of a signal <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to its Functional PCA (FPCA) transform <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> up to recentering. Although HVF/FPCA transforms are general means to obtain a different representation of signals, they also keep the usual variational characterization, i.e., taking the first <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{\ell}\}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{\ell}\}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to selecting the <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> directions maximizing signal variance under orthonormality, or minimizing reconstruction error. In the finite-dimensional case (<math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}\cong\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}\cong\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>), FPCA and PCA (transforms) are equivalent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Motivation.</span> As explained, the HVFT transform provides a principled spectral domain for (random) signals in Hilbert spaces. Their spectral representations can be directly leveraged in processing/learning algorithms as information-rich features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. However, as we elaborate more in Sec. <a href="#S4" title="4 Discretization via Bounded Operators ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and empirically show in Sec. <a href="#S5" title="5 Numerical Results ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, practically implementing FPCA requires a finite number of discretized signals. This often leads to numerical instabilities and poor transferability performance, i.e., two finite batches of discretized samples coming from the same underlying Hilbert space or from similar Hilbert spaces could potentially results in significantly different spectral representations, making FPCA-only learning extremely difficult. For these reasons, there is a clear need for a proper notion of (convolutional) filtering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> and neural networks centered on the covariance operator, to successfully merge the representational power of FPCA with the stability/transferability properties of neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Hilbert Covariance Filters and Neural Networks</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce spectral and spatial (polynomial) Hilbert coVariance Filters by building on the spectral domain induced by the covariance operator <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we leverage these filters to design Hilbert coVariance Networks.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Spectral Hilbert coVariance filter)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a function <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:[0,\left\lVert C\right\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}]\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:[0,\left\lVert C\right\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}]\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> bounded and Borel (e.g., continuous, monotone). A spectral Hilbert coVariance Filter (HVF) with frequency response <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted with <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=\mathbf{h}(C)\,x\;\triangleq\;\sum_{\ell=1}^{\infty}h(\lambda_{\ell})\,\left\langle x,\,\phi_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}\,\phi_{\ell}+h(0)\,x_{\perp}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">≜</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=\mathbf{h}(C)\,x\;\triangleq\;\sum_{\ell=1}^{\infty}h(\lambda_{\ell})\,\left\langle x,\,\phi_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}\,\phi_{\ell}+h(0)\,x_{\perp},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input and <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> is the orthogonal projection of <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By direct computation, it is trivial to check that filtering is pointwise in the HVFT domain, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{g}[\ell]\;=\;h(\lambda_{\ell})\,\widetilde{x}[\ell],\qquad\ell\geq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{g}[\ell]\;=\;h(\lambda_{\ell})\,\widetilde{x}[\ell],\qquad\ell\geq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This operation, where the frequency response <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\lambda_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\lambda_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> amplifies or attenuates the different functional principal components, is in complete analogy with the convolutions/spectral filtering in several other domains, e.g., time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, or manifolds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. By learning or designing <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\lambda_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\lambda_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>, we are able to put different weights on the different HVFT coefficients. As such, taking only the <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> variance-maximizing directions, i.e., employing the FPCA transform as a dimensionality-reduction method, is then equivalent to an ideal high/low-pass HVF (if we order the eigenvalues of the covariance operator in ascending/descending order). General spectral filters are maximally expressive, but they require explicit computation of eigenfunctions and eigenvalues. For this reason, spatial (polynomial) filters are often preferred in practice, as they are (i) scalable and stable–they avoid eigendecomposition and are eigenvector-agnostic, and (ii) sill expressive–polynomials have strong approximation capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Spatial Hilbert coVariance Filters)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given filter order <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and a set of real valued parameters <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0},\ldots,w_{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0},\ldots,w_{J}</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding spatial HVF is given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}(C)\;\triangleq\;\sum_{j=0}^{J}w_{j}\,C^{\,j}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">≜</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}(C)\;\triangleq\;\sum_{j=0}^{J}w_{j}\,C^{\,j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A spatial HVF is entirely defined by the parameters <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, using (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2 Preliminaries and Motivation ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), it is trivial to show that the frequency response of a spatial HVF has the form <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\lambda)=\sum_{k=0}^{J}w_{k}\,\lambda^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\lambda)=\sum_{k=0}^{J}w_{k}\,\lambda^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Intuitively, <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> can be thought of as a shift operator (like the time delay <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> or a graph shift <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>).
Each power <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> repeatedly spreads/smooths the signal through <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-step covariance couplings (akin to <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-hop diffusion), and the weights <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> mix these scales. We can now define Hilbert coVariance Networks using banks of HVFs and nonlinear activations.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hilbert coVariance Networks)<span id="Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A Hilbert coVariance Network (HVN) is a layered architecture composed of banks of HVFs and nonlinear activations (operators). The <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-</annotation></semantics></math>th layer considers <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> input signals <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{x_{t}^{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{F_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{x_{t}^{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{F_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> output signals <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{x_{t+1}^{u}\right\}_{u=1}^{F_{t+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{x_{t+1}^{u}\right\}_{u=1}^{F_{t+1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denoting the nonlinear activation with <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:\mathcal{H}\rightarrow\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:\mathcal{H}\rightarrow\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> , the propagation rule reads as</span></p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{t+1}^{u}=\sigma\left(\sum_{i=1}^{F_{t}}\mathbf{h}(C)_{t}^{u,i}x_{t}^{i}\right),\quad u=1,\ldots,F_{t+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></munderover><mi>𝐡</mi><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t+1}^{u}=\sigma\left(\sum_{i=1}^{F_{t}}\mathbf{h}(C)_{t}^{u,i}x_{t}^{i}\right),\quad u=1,\ldots,F_{t+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A HVN with input signals <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{x^{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{F_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{x^{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{F_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is then built as the stack of <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> layers defined as in (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In Definition 3 (Hilbert coVariance Networks). ‣ 3 Hilbert Covariance Filters and Neural Networks ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), where <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{i}=x^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{i}=x^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. To globally represent the HVN, we collect all the filters in <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}=\left\{h_{t}^{u,i}\right\}_{t,u,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}=\left\{h_{t}^{u,i}\right\}_{t,u,i}</annotation></semantics></math> and describe the HVN outputs as a mapping <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{T}^{u}\}_{u=1}^{F_{T}}=\bm{\Psi}\left(\mathcal{W},C,\left\{x^{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{F_{0}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝚿</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{T}^{u}\}_{u=1}^{F_{T}}=\bm{\Psi}\left(\mathcal{W},C,\left\{x^{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{F_{0}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> to enhance that it is parameterized by filters <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and the covariance operator <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. An HVN can be spectral or spatial, depending on the type of employed filters.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Discretization via Bounded Operators</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hilbert coVariance Filters and Neural Networks operate on signals in a (possibly infinite-dimensional) Hilbert space whose distribution—and thus the covariance operator <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>—is unknown, so they are not directly implementable. We make them practical in two steps: (i) working with the empirical covariance operator using i.i.d., and (ii) discretizing the samples using bounded discretization operators to enable finite-dimensional processing and learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical covariance operator.</span>
Given i.i.d. samples <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{x}=n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{x}=n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The empirical covariance operator <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{n}:\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{n}:\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined, for <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, by</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{n}v\;\triangleq\;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left\langle x_{i}-\bar{x},\,v\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}\,(x_{i}-\bar{x}),\quad v\in\mathcal{H}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≜</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{n}v\;\triangleq\;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left\langle x_{i}-\bar{x},\,v\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}\,(x_{i}-\bar{x}),\quad v\in\mathcal{H}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">By definition, <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded, self -adjoint, positive, and finite rank with <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r:=\operatorname{rank}(\hat{C}_{n})\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r:=\operatorname{rank}(\hat{C}_{n})\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence there exist eigenvalues <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\lambda}_{1}\geq\cdots\geq\hat{\lambda}_{r}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\lambda}_{1}\geq\cdots\geq\hat{\lambda}_{r}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (with <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>) and orthonormal eigenfunctions <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{\phi}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\phi}_{r}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{\phi}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\phi}_{r}\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{n}v\;=\;\sum_{\ell=1}^{r}\hat{\lambda}_{\ell}\,\left\langle v,\,\hat{\phi}_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}\,\hat{\phi}_{\ell},\qquad v\in\mathcal{H}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{n}v\;=\;\sum_{\ell=1}^{r}\hat{\lambda}_{\ell}\,\left\langle v,\,\hat{\phi}_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}\,\hat{\phi}_{\ell},\qquad v\in\mathcal{H}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalue may have infinite multiplicity with null spaces given by <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v\in\mathcal{H}:\left\langle v,\,\hat{\phi}_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}=0\ \forall\,\ell\leq r\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.667em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v\in\mathcal{H}:\left\langle v,\,\hat{\phi}_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}=0\ \forall\,\ell\leq r\}</annotation></semantics></math>. As a valuable expressivity analysis of spatial HVFs, we now show that their empirical counterpart, i.e., replacing <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Definition 2 (Spatial Hilbert coVariance Filters). ‣ 3 Hilbert Covariance Filters and Neural Networks ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), can provably recover the (empirical) FPCA transform of the samples. Let <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{\lambda}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\lambda}_{r}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{\lambda}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\lambda}_{r}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the positive eigenvalues of <math id="S4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> (with multiplicity) and <math id="S4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{\ell}\}_{\ell=1}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{\ell}\}_{\ell=1}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding orthonormal family. For a distinct eigenvalue value <math id="S4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\alpha)=\{\ell\leq r:\hat{\lambda}_{\ell}=\alpha\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\alpha)=\{\ell\leq r:\hat{\lambda}_{\ell}=\alpha\}</annotation></semantics></math> and define the orthogonal projector <math id="S4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\alpha}:\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\alpha}:\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\alpha}x\;\triangleq\;\sum_{\ell\in I(\alpha)}\left\langle x,\,\hat{\phi}_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}\,\hat{\phi}_{\ell},\qquad x\in\mathcal{H}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">≜</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\alpha}x\;\triangleq\;\sum_{\ell\in I(\alpha)}\left\langle x,\,\hat{\phi}_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}\,\hat{\phi}_{\ell},\qquad x\in\mathcal{H}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda=\{\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{q}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda=\{\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{q}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (with <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\leq r</annotation></semantics></math>) be the set of <em id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">distinct</em> positive eigenvalues of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a spatial HVF <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Definition 2 (Spatial Hilbert coVariance Filters). ‣ 3 Hilbert Covariance Filters and Neural Networks ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\alpha}(\hat{C}_{n})\,x\;=\;P_{\alpha}x,\qquad x\in\mathcal{H}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\alpha}(\hat{C}_{n})\,x\;=\;P_{\alpha}x,\qquad x\in\mathcal{H}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, one can choose <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(\mathbf{h}_{\alpha})\leq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(\mathbf{h}_{\alpha})\leq q</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span> See Appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and any orthonormal basis <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{\phi}_{\ell}\}_{\ell\in I(\alpha)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{\phi}_{\ell}\}_{\ell\in I(\alpha)}</annotation></semantics></math> of its eigenspace. For any <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a polynomial HVF <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Definition 2 (Spatial Hilbert coVariance Filters). ‣ 3 Hilbert Covariance Filters and Neural Networks ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\langle\,\mathbf{h}_{\alpha}(\hat{C}_{n})x\,,\,\hat{\phi}_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}\,=\,\left\langle x,\,\hat{\phi}_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}},\qquad\forall\,\ell\in I(\alpha)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\langle\,\mathbf{h}_{\alpha}(\hat{C}_{n})x\,,\,\hat{\phi}_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}\,=\,\left\langle x,\,\hat{\phi}_{\ell}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}},\qquad\forall\,\ell\in I(\alpha),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e., recovering the <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-th (empirical) FPCA score of <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Discretization Operator)<span id="Thmdefinition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{j}\}_{j=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{j}\}_{j=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}:\mathcal{H}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}:\mathcal{H}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be bounded linear functionals on <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the Riesz representation theorem, there exist <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}(x)=\left\langle x,\,s_{j}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}(x)=\left\langle x,\,s_{j}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. The discretization operator <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}:\mathcal{H}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}:\mathcal{H}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}x=[f_{1}(x),\ldots,f_{m}(x)]^{\top}=[\left\langle x,\,s_{1}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}},\ldots,\left\langle x,\,s_{m}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}]^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}x=[f_{1}(x),\ldots,f_{m}(x)]^{\top}=[\left\langle x,\,s_{1}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}},\ldots,\left\langle x,\,s_{m}\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}]^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Hilbert adjoint <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}^{*}:\mathbb{R}^{m}\to\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}^{*}:\mathbb{R}^{m}\to\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\langle x,\,S_{m}^{*}a\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}=\langle S_{m}x,a\rangle_{\mathbb{R}^{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\langle x,\,S_{m}^{*}a\right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}=\langle S_{m}x,a\rangle_{\mathbb{R}^{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> and it acts on <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}^{*}a\;=\;\sum_{j=1}^{m}a_{j}\,s_{j},\qquad a=[a_{1},\ldots,a_{m}]^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}^{*}a\;=\;\sum_{j=1}^{m}a_{j}\,s_{j},\qquad a=[a_{1},\ldots,a_{m}]^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> we denote <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}^{(m)}:=S_{m}x_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}^{(m)}:=S_{m}x_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{x}^{(m)}=\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i}x_{i}^{(m)}=S_{m}\bar{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{x}^{(m)}=\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i}x_{i}^{(m)}=S_{m}\bar{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the empirical covariance matrix of the discretized signals is given by</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{n}^{(m)}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\big(x_{i}^{(m)}-\overline{x}^{(m)}\big)\big(x_{i}^{(m)}-\overline{x}^{(m)}\big)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{n}^{(m)}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\big(x_{i}^{(m)}-\overline{x}^{(m)}\big)\big(x_{i}^{(m)}-\overline{x}^{(m)}\big)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">As a sanity check for the empirical covariance matrix, the following result shows that the discretized version of the empirical covariance operator is the empirical covariance matrix. In other words, discretizing the samples and then computing the empirical covariance matrix is equivalent to computing the empirical covariance operator and then discretizing it.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{n}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{n}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math> be a discretization operator as in (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In Definition 4 (Discretization Operator). ‣ 4 Discretization via Bounded Operators ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), the empirical covariance operator as in (<a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4 Discretization via Bounded Operators ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), and the empirical covariance matrix as in (<a href="#S4.E15" title="In 4 Discretization via Bounded Operators ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), respectively. The following holds:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\;\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{n}^{(m)}=S_{m}\,\hat{C}_{n}\,S_{m}^{*}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\;\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{n}^{(m)}=S_{m}\,\hat{C}_{n}\,S_{m}^{*}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span> See Appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discrete HVFs, and HVNs.</span> Once signals are discretized and the empirical covariance matrix is computed, discrete HVFs and HVNs are simply given by (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Definition 2 (Spatial Hilbert coVariance Filters). ‣ 3 Hilbert Covariance Filters and Neural Networks ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In Definition 3 (Hilbert coVariance Networks). ‣ 3 Hilbert Covariance Filters and Neural Networks ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) with the covariance operator replaced by the empirical covariance matrix and the signals replaced by their discretized version, i.e. <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x^{(m),u}_{T}\}_{u=1}^{F_{T}}=\bm{\Psi}(\mathcal{W},\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{n}^{(m)},\{x^{(m)}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝚿</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x^{(m),u}_{T}\}_{u=1}^{F_{T}}=\bm{\Psi}(\mathcal{W},\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{n}^{(m)},\{x^{(m)}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Discretized HVNs are principled instances of coVariance Neural Network (VNN) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and, as a consequence, of Graph (convolutional) Neural Networks (GNNs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. To better enhance this similarity, we rewrite the layer in (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In Definition 3 (Hilbert coVariance Networks). ‣ 3 Hilbert Covariance Filters and Neural Networks ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) in matrix form by introducing the matrices <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{(m)}_{t}=\{x_{t,u}^{(m)}\}_{u=1}^{F_{t}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times F_{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{(m)}_{t}=\{x_{t,u}^{(m)}\}_{u=1}^{F_{t}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times F_{l}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{t,j}=\{w_{t,j}^{u,q}\}_{q=1,u=1}^{F_{l},F_{l+1}}\in\mathbb{R}^{F_{l}\times F_{l+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{t,j}=\{w_{t,j}^{u,q}\}_{q=1,u=1}^{F_{l},F_{l+1}}\in\mathbb{R}^{F_{l}\times F_{l+1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as</p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{(m)}_{t+1}=\sigma\Bigg(\sum_{j=0}^{J}\Big(\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{n}^{(m)}\Big)^{j}\mathbf{X}_{t}^{(m)}\mathbf{W}_{t,j}\Bigg)\;\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times F_{l+1}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{(m)}_{t+1}=\sigma\Bigg(\sum_{j=0}^{J}\Big(\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{n}^{(m)}\Big)^{j}\mathbf{X}_{t}^{(m)}\mathbf{W}_{t,j}\Bigg)\;\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times F_{l+1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where the filter weights <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{W}_{t,j}\}_{t,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{W}_{t,j}\}_{t,j}</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable parameters, and <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{(m)}_{0}=\{x_{i}^{(m)}\}_{i=1}^{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{(m)}_{0}=\{x_{i}^{(m)}\}_{i=1}^{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Use-Cases for (Discrete) HVNs.</span> To showcase the versatility of our framework, in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Use-Cases for HVNs ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> we illustrate three practical <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{H},S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{H},S)</annotation></semantics></math> pairs together with admissible nonlinear activations <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, we describe how HVNs can be applied to functional data (e.g. time series, as we also show in Sec. <a href="#S5" title="5 Numerical Results ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), infinite sequences, and data defined over Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Spaces.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.fig1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13178v1/acc_vs_n.svg" id="S4.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/274;" width="476" height="274"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Test accuracy versus number of samples.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13178v1/acc_vs_snr.svg" id="S4.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/275;" width="476" height="275"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Test accuracy versus SNR (in dB).</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F1.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13178v1/ecg.svg" id="S4.F1.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/276;" width="476" height="276"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S4.F1.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Test accuracy on ECG dataset.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Numerical Results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate HVNs on a synthetic and a real task.
In the synthetic setting, we perform binary classification of multivariate time-series bags.
The time-series are evaluated on the unit interval and have <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> channels, i.e., they live in <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}=L^{2}([0,1],\beta;\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}=L^{2}([0,1],\beta;\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is the Lebesgue measure. We observe them through the discretization operator <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> as defined in Ex. 1 of Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Use-Cases for HVNs ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, i.e., we perform channelwise bin–averaging on a partition of <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> intervals, yielding <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=dp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=dp</annotation></semantics></math> discrete components per sample. For a fixed class <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th sample <math id="S5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from a zero–mean Gaussian process with matrix–valued kernel <math id="S5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{(y)}(t,s)=k_{t}(t,s)\Sigma_{c}^{(y)}\ \in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{(y)}(t,s)=k_{t}(t,s)\Sigma_{c}^{(y)}\ \in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t,s\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t,s\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Equivalently, the covariance operator from (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Preliminaries and Motivation ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is given by</p>
<table id="S5.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C^{(y)}v)(t)=\int_{0}^{1}K^{(y)}(t,s)v(s)ds,\qquad v\in\mathcal{H}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C^{(y)}v)(t)=\int_{0}^{1}K^{(y)}(t,s)v(s)ds,\qquad v\in\mathcal{H}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">HVNs leverage the whole covariance structure of the data. This means that, if the process is stationary, i.e., <math id="S5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}(t,s)=k_{t}(|t-s|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}(t,s)=k_{t}(|t-s|)</annotation></semantics></math>, they are able to learn both from second order moments (the diagonal entries of the compressed covariance matrices) that are dependent only on a single channel at a certain time lag, and mixed second order moments (the off diagonal entries of the compressed covariance matrices) that are jointly dependent on all the channels at a certain time lag. For this reason, to structure a classification task whose discriminant elements can be captured by HVNs and not necessarily by models that only act component-wise, e.g., a linear model or an MLP, we deliberately share the temporal kernel across classes and make the label depend only on cross–channel correlation, i.e.:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{t}(t,s)^{(0)}=k_{t}(t,s)^{(1)}=\exp\Big(-\frac{(t-s)^{2}}{2\phi^{2}}\Big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{t}(t,s)^{(0)}=k_{t}(t,s)^{(1)}=\exp\Big(-\frac{(t-s)^{2}}{2\phi^{2}}\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textrm{and }\qquad\Sigma_{c}^{(0)}=I_{d},\qquad\Sigma_{c}^{(1)}=\big[\rho^{|i-j|}\big]_{i,j=1}^{d}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>and </mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textrm{and }\qquad\Sigma_{c}^{(0)}=I_{d},\qquad\Sigma_{c}^{(1)}=\big[\rho^{|i-j|}\big]_{i,j=1}^{d},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi,\rho&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi,\rho&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this task, a data point with class <math id="S5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a bag of <math id="S5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. samples drawn from
<math id="S5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dots,x_{n}\ \stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{i.i.d.}}}{{\sim}}\ \mathsf{GP}\big(0,K^{(y)}\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mover><mo>∼</mo><mtext>i.i.d.</mtext></mover><mrow><mi>𝖦𝖯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dots,x_{n}\ \stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{i.i.d.}}}{{\sim}}\ \mathsf{GP}\big(0,K^{(y)}\big),</annotation></semantics></math>
discretized as noisy <math id="S5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}^{(m)}:=S_{m}x_{i}+\epsilon\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}^{(m)}:=S_{m}x_{i}+\epsilon\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is AWGN, and centered by <math id="S5.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{x}^{(m)}=\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{x}^{(m)}=\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math>. We compare HVNs against two baselines acting on the same discretized bags: (i) MLP, obtained by simply setting <math id="S5.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{n}^{(m)}=I_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{n}^{(m)}=I_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In Definition 3 (Hilbert coVariance Networks). ‣ 3 Hilbert Covariance Filters and Neural Networks ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), and (ii) FPCA, consisting of computing the first <math id="S5.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> discrete FPCA scores (i.e., the PCA scores of the discrete <math id="S5.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}^{(m)}\}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}^{(m)}\}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in the bag). For all the tested models, the output of the last layer (for HVN and MLP) or the scores (for PCA) are fed to an MLP classifier to get the class probabilities.
We set <math id="S5.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi=0.20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi=0.20</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.7</annotation></semantics></math>, train with ADAM optimizer to minimize cross–entropy loss, we normalize the compressed covariance matrices for training stability (for HVN), and we use GELU nonlinearity (for HVN and MLP), mean pooling, and a polynomial degree (for HVN) <math id="S5.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=2</annotation></semantics></math>. For both HVN and MLP, we use 2 layers, 32 output signals in each HVN layer, and we adjust the MLP hidden dimensions to match the number of parameters of HVN. We fix <math id="S5.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=32</annotation></semantics></math>, and we generate balanced train/test sets for different numbers of samples <math id="S5.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and signal to noise ratios <math id="S5.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SNR_{dB}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SNR_{dB}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined, given a bag, as <math id="S5.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\log_{10}\left((1/n)\sum_{i=1}^{n}\|x_{i}^{(m)}\|^{2}/\textrm{var}(\epsilon)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mtext>var</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\log_{10}\left((1/n)\sum_{i=1}^{n}\|x_{i}^{(m)}\|^{2}/\textrm{var}(\epsilon)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In Figures <a href="#S4.F1.sf1" title="In 4 Discretization via Bounded Operators ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>-<a href="#S4.F1.sf2" title="In 4 Discretization via Bounded Operators ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a> we show the test accuracy vs <math id="S5.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> (for fixed <math id="S5.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SNR_{dB}=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SNR_{dB}=30</annotation></semantics></math>) and vs <math id="S5.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SNR_{dB}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SNR_{dB}</annotation></semantics></math> (for fixed <math id="S5.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=24</annotation></semantics></math>), respectively. The MLP achieves mediocre accuracies (and is prone to overfitting) as it never mixes the <math id="S5.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> components of the samples, accessing only first–order information, whereas the label is encoded in cross–feature covariance. The FPCA baseline gives random accuracy as it rotates each bag to its variance–maximizing basis, leading to coordinate drift across samples (sign flips, permutations,…), which a single classifier must reconcile. In contrast, HVN shows strong performance thanks to the transferable nature of HVFs and their ability to capture discriminative second–order structure consistently across bags. We further evaluate HVNs on the ECG5000 dataset from the UCR repository <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, which contains 5000 ECG samples of length 140 and 5 classes denoting the heartbeat type.
We again compare HVNs, MLP, and FPCA with bin averaging and varying discretization resolution <math id="S5.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. The hyperparameters are set as in the synthetic experiment. In this case, the goal is to classify individual time-series and not (possibly streaming) bags; thus, both for HVNN and FPCA, we compute the empirical covariance matrix on the entire training (and test, at inference) set and then use it to classify the individual time-series. In this way, both HVNN and FPCA have a more robust estimate of the covariance operator. Indeed, Figure <a href="#S4.F1.sf3" title="In 4 Discretization via Bounded Operators ‣ CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(c)</span></a> shows that HVN and FPCA outperform MLP across all values of <math id="S5.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating the importance of covariance information. HVN outperforms both MLP and FPCA by a margin, especially for increasing <math id="S5.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, corroborating its capability to incorporate covariance information into its end-to-end learning dynamics robustly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We proposed a novel convolutional learning framework for signals in (possibly infinite-dimensional) Hilbert spaces, built around the (empirical) covariance operator.
The generality of our framework suggests several future directions. We will study the convergence properties of discrete HVFs/HVNs to their infinite-dimensional counterparts as the discretization resolution becomes infinitely fine-grained. Using these results, we will analyze the transferability properties of HVNs.</p>
</div>
</section>
